🗓️ WEEK 9 — REPLACING THE QR SCANNER WITH A BARCODE READER

Date: Monday, 5 October 2026

🎯 Objective

The objective of Week 9 was to start Iteration 3 by fixing the scanner that failed in the mid-semester demo, so that it reads the real barcodes printed on retail tags.

🛠️ Work Completed
Removed the html5-qrcode library.
Used the browser's built-in BarcodeDetector API (Chrome).
Added a fallback for Safari and Firefox:
barcode-detector package
zxing-wasm (ZXing-C++ compiled to WebAssembly)
Served the WebAssembly file from our own server and loaded it only when the scanner opens.
Opened the rear camera at 1920 × 1080 with getUserMedia.
Added a torch button, a beep, a vibration and an Undo button for each scan.
Added an HTTPS mode for testing on phones: npm run dev:https.
Added a home screen so the camera does not open straight after login.

⚠️ Error / Problem Faced

The QR code printed on the same tag was still being read instead of the barcode.

Tags held off-centre were missed, but scanning the whole frame every time was slow.

On phones the camera would not open at http://<laptop IP>, because browsers allow the camera only on HTTPS.

One scan sometimes added the same item twice.

✅ Resolution
Allowed only linear barcode formats (EAN-13, EAN-8, UPC, Code 128, Code 39, ITF) and turned QR off.
Scanned a wide band in the middle of the frame every 90 ms, and the full frame every 4th pass.
Ran the dev server over HTTPS so the phone camera works.
Ignored the same code for 2.2 seconds after a successful scan.
Result: one read takes 25–35 ms per frame.

🖼️ Project Picture

Product labels page with EAN-13 barcodes for every product, used to test the scanner from another screen.

💡 Learning

We learned to choose a library that fits the real input: shop tags have barcodes, not QR codes.

We also learned that browsers only allow the camera on secure (HTTPS) pages.

